'''Acereries de l'Istme, S.A.''' ({{lang|ca|Acereries de l'Istme}}; "Isthmus Steelworks"), commonly abbreviated '''ACI''', is a Balboan {{w|steel}} company and the largest steel producer in [[Balboa]]. The company operates the Les Drassanes steelworks in the heavy industry municipality of [[Les Drassanes]], northwest of [[Portnou]], which is Balboa's only {{w|integrated steel plant|integrated steel mill}} — combining {{w|blast furnace}}s, {{w|basic oxygen steelmaking|basic oxygen furnaces}}, and {{w|electric arc furnace}}s. ACI is the direct successor to the [[Foneria General de Balboa]] (FGB), founded in 1903, making it the oldest steel company in the country.

ACI is listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] (SCX: ACI) and is a constituent of the [[SCX 30]] index. It is the only steel company in the SCX 30. The company produces approximately 8.0 million tonnes of {{w|crude steel}} per year, accounting for 56 per cent of total Balboan steel production.

== History ==

=== Foneria General de Balboa (1903–1949) ===

{{main|Foneria General de Balboa}}

ACI traces its origins to 1903, when '''Esteve Camps i Ribera''', a Volisanian metallurgist from the colonial settler community, established the '''[[Foneria General de Balboa]]''' (FGB) — the General Foundry of Balboa — at [[Les Drassanes]], the shipbuilding and heavy industry municipality northwest of [[Portnou]]. Camps had previously worked at a Volisanian ironworks in [[Ampuria]] and recognised that the [[Zong Balboa]] administration's expansion of the [[Balboa Canal|canal zone]] would require a domestic source of steel for naval shipbuilding, dockyard construction, and military infrastructure.

The FGB was established with the backing of the Zong Balboa colonial administration, which granted the company land at Les Drassanes and preferential access to Zong naval contracts. The iron works initially operated a single {{w|blast furnace}} using {{w|iron ore}} imported from [[Louyang]] — then a Zong dependency with substantial iron ore deposits — and {{w|coking coal}} imported from [[Kingdom of Zong|Zong]]. The company produced basic {{w|pig iron}}, {{w|cast iron}}, and small quantities of {{w|carbon steel|mild steel}} for the Zong navy.

During the 1910s and 1920s, the FGB expanded with the addition of a second {{w|blast furnace}} and an {{w|open hearth furnace}} shop. The company's workforce grew from approximately 200 in 1903 to over 1,500 by 1930, including both Volisanian settlers and Black Balboan workers recruited from the surrounding Estret province. The FGB's production reached approximately 150,000 tonnes of crude steel per year by the mid-1930s.

The [[Great Adonian War]] (1935–1947) devastated the FGB. The Les Drassanes iron works was severely damaged during the fighting — the blast furnaces were sabotaged by retreating Zong forces in 1947, and the open hearth shop was destroyed by aerial bombardment. By the end of the war, the FGB's production had fallen to zero and the company's Volisanian management had fled.

=== Acereria Nacional de Balboa (1949–1982) ===

{{main|Acereria Nacional de Balboa}}

In 1949, the [[Adonian Community]] provided reconstruction loans to restart the Balboan steel industry. The Balboan rump state government contributed matching funds, and the two parties established the '''[[Acereria Nacional de Balboa]]''' (ANB) — the National Steel Company of Balboa — as a state-owned conglomerate. The ANB absorbed the ruins of the FGB and began rebuilding the Les Drassanes works with modern equipment imported from Volisania and Breisland.

The ANB's rebuilt Les Drassanes works began production in 1953, during the [[Adonian Community]] mandate period (1950–1960), with a single {{w|blast furnace}} and a new {{w|basic oxygen steelmaking|basic oxygen furnace}} (BOF) shop. The company also established a second steelworks at [[Sant Bart]], the rump state capital in the interior highlands, using a small {{w|electric arc furnace}} (EAF) powered by hydropower from the nearby [[Yalabirga]] river system.

After reunification in 1973, the ANB invested in modernisation, expanding the Les Drassanes works with a second blast furnace and upgrading the Sant Bart EAF capacity. By 1980, the ANB produced approximately 3.5 million tonnes of crude steel per year.

=== Privatisation and independence (1982–present) ===

In 1982, as part of the broader privatisation programme, the Balboan government privatised the ANB. The company was restructured and split into two independent companies: ACI, which inherited the Les Drassanes works, and [[Ferro Balboa]] (FBL), which inherited the Sant Bart EAF plant. ACI was listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] in 1983. The [[Camps family]], descendants of the original founder Esteve Camps i Ribera, acquired a 10 per cent stake in the privatisation.

The 1990s and 2000s saw significant growth. ACI expanded its Les Drassanes works, adding a third blast furnace and a second BOF shop, and modernised its flat product rolling mills. The company also added three {{w|electric arc furnace}}s to complement its blast furnace operations, allowing it to process scrap metal alongside the integrated route.

The [[Adonian pandemic]] of 2020 temporarily reduced demand from the construction sector but increased demand from the packaging and appliance sectors. ACI maintained production through the pandemic, supported by the domestic construction boom that followed the Balboan government's infrastructure stimulus programme.

By 2026, ACI produced approximately 8.0 million tonnes of crude steel per year and employed approximately 8,000 people.

== Operations ==

ACI operates the Les Drassanes steelworks, Balboa's only integrated steel plant. The facility is located in the heavy industry municipality of [[Les Drassanes]], northwest of [[Portnou]], and comprises:

* '''2 × {{w|blast furnace}}s''': Produce {{w|pig iron}} from imported {{w|iron ore}} and {{w|coking coal}}. The blast furnaces are the only ones in Balboa.
* '''2 × {{w|basic oxygen steelmaking|basic oxygen furnaces}} (BOF)''': Convert pig iron from the blast furnaces into {{w|crude steel}}.
* '''3 × {{w|electric arc furnace}}s (EAF)''': Produce crude steel from {{w|scrap metal}}, complementing the integrated route.
* '''{{w|rolling (metalworking)|Rolling mill}} complex''': Produces both long and flat products.

The Les Drassanes location provides direct access to the bulk cargo facilities of the [[Port de Portnou]] for importing iron ore and coking coal, and to the E21 expressway and rail corridors for distributing finished steel to domestic markets. The plant draws power from the national grid, which is predominantly hydroelectric. ACI's blast furnaces also use {{w|metallurgical coke}} as a fuel and reducing agent, which is produced from imported coking coal at the on-site coking plant.

== Products ==

ACI's product mix includes both long and flat products:

=== Long products ===

* '''{{w|Rebar|Reinforcing bar}} (rebar)''': Used in construction and infrastructure projects.
* '''{{w|Wire rod}}''': Used in construction, fasteners, and wire products.
* '''{{w|Steel bar|Merchant bar}}''': Used in fabrication and structural applications.

=== Flat products ===

* '''{{w|Hot rolling|Hot-rolled coil}}''': Used in manufacturing, pipes, and construction.
* '''{{w|Cold rolling|Cold-rolled coil}}''': Used in automotive, appliances, and packaging.
* '''{{w|Steel plate|Plate}}''': Used in shipbuilding, pressure vessels, and structural steel.

ACI is the only Balboan producer of flat products, which are used in the manufacturing, automotive, and shipbuilding sectors.

== Raw materials ==

ACI is the only Balboan steelmaker that requires {{w|iron ore}} and {{w|coking coal}}, as its blast furnaces use the integrated steelmaking route. The EAF operations use {{w|scrap metal}}.

* '''Iron ore''': Imported from [[Tapuya]] (approximately 80 per cent) and [[Lacashe]] (approximately 20 per cent). Iron ore is shipped to the bulk cargo terminals at the [[Port de Portnou]] and transported by rail to the Les Drassanes works.
* '''Coking coal''': Imported from [[Tapuya]] and [[Lacashe]]. Processed into {{w|metallurgical coke}} at the on-site coking plant.
* '''Scrap metal''': Sourced from domestic collection (approximately 60 per cent) and imports from [[Potocsí]] and [[Tapuya]] (approximately 40 per cent).
* '''{{w|Graphite electrode}}s''': Supplied by [[Shoko Denko Balboa]] (SD Balboa), which produces synthetic graphite from [[petroleum coke]] — a byproduct of [[CIEA]]'s [[Badia Curta]] refinery.

== Ownership ==

ACI is publicly traded on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]]. The ownership structure reflects the company's history as a colonial-era foundry that was state-owned and then privatised:

* '''[[Camps family]]''': 10% — descendants of founder Esteve Camps i Ribera; represented on the board
* '''[[Fons d'Inversió Directa de Balboa]] (FIDB)''': 8% — institutional investor
* '''[[Balboa Pension Fund]] (BPF)''': 5% — institutional anchor investor
* '''Public float''': approximately 77% — held by institutional and retail investors

